{"archetypes":[{"name":"bash-parity","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Large Python reimplementation claims behavioral parity with bash finalize/ship paths, which needs specialist scrutiny beyond generic correctness.","prompt_body":"Investigate whether the Python finalize, ship, merge, run-log, and CI-monitor changes preserve the observable bash status tokens, branch outcomes, and warning-versus-fatal behavior described by the plan. Pay particular attention to postbump, postmerge, teardown, recovery gates, and parity tests that may accidentally assert Python behavior instead of bash behavior. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"git-safety","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff touches rebases, force-pushes, branch deletion, and cleanup flows where small mistakes can damage repositories.","prompt_body":"Review all git operation paths for unsafe pushes, stale remote assumptions, wrong-remote targeting, branch deletion hazards, dirty-tree handling, and incorrect retry or abort behavior. Check that lease-based force-push, pending rebase retry, local cleanup, and postmerge sentinel logic cannot move or delete the wrong branch. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"runlog-recovery","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Run-log manifest recovery and fail-closed semantics are central acceptance criteria and affect postmerge, merge, and teardown flows.","prompt_body":"Inspect manifest recovery, partial status tagging, recovery_ok propagation, final report ordering, and commit/skip behavior across run_logs, ship, merge, and finalize. Verify missing manifest and missing run-directory cases do not silently write done status, render reports, or commit logs when recovery should fail closed. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"timing-ledger","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff adds round-timing emission across design and implement workflows, which has ordering and idempotency risks not covered by static reviewers.","prompt_body":"Investigate the timing-ledger changes for duplicate rows, missing terminal rows, wrong ledger paths, stale environment leakage, and inconsistent design versus implement phase labels. Check the new shell helpers and harnesses around plan-review and Step 5 round timing, especially deferred emission paths after lint-fix or main-agent handoff. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"state-resume","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"New resume state plumbing spans RunContext, ship state serialization, monitor results, and CI-fix retry loops.","prompt_body":"Trace CI_FIX_REBASE_PENDING and related resume state from environment and state-file hydration through monitor evaluation, fix results, ship-state writes, and subsequent retry or clear points. Look for state lost across with_ copies, stale context writes, inconsistent bool parsing, or paths that proceed to PR creation with pending retry state unresolved. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"shell-portability","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":1,"rationale":"Many new and edited shell scripts must remain portable to the repository's bash and lint conventions.","prompt_body":"Review new and modified shell scripts for bash 3.2 compatibility, quoting, unset variable safety, awk/grep portability, trap cleanup, sourced-script side effects, and quiet-output conventions. Pay special attention to timing helper scripts, design round artifact scripts, and review-and-fix loop changes that run in constrained harness environments. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
